4. Actual installation commands
The following is the proposed implementation using Ubuntu, native Ollama and Docker-based Open WebUI.
Step 1 – Launch EC2
In AWS Console:
1. Navigate to EC2 → Launch Instance.
2. Name: ollama-llm-server.
3. AMI: Ubuntu Server 24.04 LTS.
4. Instance type: g5.xlarge.
5. Storage: 100 GB gp3.
6. Create or select an SSH key pair.
Security group:
Port	Purpose	Access
22	SSH	Your public IP only
3000	Open WebUI (initial testing)	Your public IP only
11434	Ollama API	Do not expose publicly
443	HTTPS (later)	Authorized users

Step 2 – Connect to EC2
ssh -i your-key.pem ubuntu@EC2-PUBLIC-IP


Update Ubuntu:
sudo apt update
sudo apt upgrade -y


Step 3 – Install and verify NVIDIA drivers
Install the recommended NVIDIA driver:
sudo ubuntu-drivers install


Reboot:
sudo reboot


Reconnect and verify:
nvidia-smi


You should see the NVIDIA A10G GPU and its memory.

For Docker-based GPU workloads, install NVIDIA Container Toolkit as well. Ollama running natively can use the installed NVIDIA drivers directly.
Step 4 – Install Ollama
curl -fsSL https://ollama.com/install.sh | sh


Check:
ollama --version


Download Llama:
ollama pull llama3.1:8b


Run the model:
ollama run llama3.1:8b


