Step 5 – Configure Ollama for Open WebUI
Ollama normally listens on localhost. We need to allow the Open WebUI Docker container to communicate with it.

Create a systemd override:
sudo systemctl edit ollama


Add:
[Service]
Environment="OLLAMA_HOST=0.0.0.0:11434"


Save and exit.

Restart Ollama:
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl restart ollama


Verify:
curl http://localhost:11434/api/tags


You should see the downloaded Llama model.

Security note: Do not add port 11434 to the EC2 security group's inbound rules. We will use it internally between the services. 
docs.openwebui.com+1

Step 6 – Install Docker and Open WebUI
Install Docker:
sudo apt install docker.io -y


Start Docker:
sudo systemctl enable --now docker


Create persistent storage:
sudo docker volume create open-webui


Start Open WebUI:
sudo docker run -d \
  -p 3000:8080 \
  --add-host=host.docker.internal:host-gateway \
  -e OLLAMA_BASE_URL=http://host.docker.internal:11434 \
  -e WEBUI_SECRET_KEY="$(openssl rand -hex 32)" \
  -v open-webui:/app/backend/data \
  --name open-webui \
  --restart always \
  ghcr.io/open-webui/open-webui:main


This configuration:
- Runs Open WebUI in a Docker container.
- Connects it to Ollama running on the EC2 host.
- Persists chat history and user data.
- Enables the WebUI authentication mechanism.
This follows the official Open WebUI deployment approach. 
